{
  "rules": [
    "Horizon-dependent bias: forecasts are slightly high in the first ~1–10 days (often by ~0–1.5), but become increasingly low beyond ~day 15, with the largest underprediction in the last third of the horizon (days ~20–30).",
    "Trend underreaction: when the market transitions into a sustained uptrend, the model lags and fails to extrapolate the drift; apply a positive trend correction that increases with horizon (e.g., add an upward ramp after day ~10 if recent 10–15d slope is positive).",
    "Late-horizon upside tail miss: the model systematically misses sharp upside extensions/rallies (true outcomes accelerate to the mid-70s while forecasts stay high-60s/low-70s); add an upside convexity term when recent volatility is rising and price is making higher highs.",
    "Over-smoothing around turning points: the model dampens both drawdowns and rebounds, producing paths that are too flat; increase responsiveness (wider forecast band / larger step changes) when the last 5–10 days show consecutive directional moves.",
    "Mean-reversion miscalibration: the model reverts too strongly toward a ~68–70 anchor, which causes underprediction during breakouts and mild overprediction during short-lived dips; reduce mean-reversion strength when price is above recent moving averages and momentum is positive."
  ]
}
